## The writing

I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: "Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``: reasoning prose before or after the answer frequently contains stray braces, and an object nested inside another is not a candidate of its own. The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text." The two reply texts are:

Text 1: {"a": 1} [{"b": 2}]
Text 2: [{"b": 2}]

For Text 1, the rule requires the answer {"a": 1}, because that is the only top-level object; the object {"b": 2} is nested inside an array and is not a candidate of its own. For Text 2, the rule requires the function to raise RecordError, because there is no top-level object at all. The check, as implemented, returns {"b": 2} for both texts: its top-level scan starts a raw_decode at every '{' without checking whether the brace is inside an array, so it treats the nested object as a candidate and, since it is the last candidate, returns it. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if running the function on Text 1 returns {"a": 1} or raises, or if running it on Text 2 raises RecordError, because then the check would not give the same answer on the two texts.

